{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HpygezVMsz5PJV68oqHwau","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose the next task.\n\n## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix utilities as a test bed. It derives formal specifications from C source code, generates Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verifies parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: \n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/sys/` - Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Shared output formatting\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n\n\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specifications (human-authored, read-only for generation)\n\n**Current Implementation State** (from source_code):\n- `pkg/testutils/`, `pkg/sys/`, `pkg/format/` all exist with working code\n- `cmd/cat/`, `cmd/wc/`, `cmd/sponge/`, `cmd/du/`, and `cmd/true/` are already implemented\n- `cmd/version/` also exists\n\n**Releases in scope**: 00.0, 01.0, 01.1, 01.2, 01.3, 02.0, 02.1, 02.2\n\nThere's a discrepancy between the code_status file and what's actually in the source code. The status file marks several releases as \"code not started\" (00.0 through 01.3), but the source_code shows these packages and commands are already implemented. I need to reconcile this mismatch to understand the true state of the project.\n\nLooking at what's already been built: testutils, sys, format, cat, wc, sponge, du, true, and version are all present in the codebase. Issue #204 for implementing cmd/true is currently in progress, which makes sense since that code already exists. Now I need to figure out what actually remains to be done across these releases.\n\nFor Release 02.0, I see that true has code but its spec is still pending, false has an orphaned PRD that needs attention, and basename, dirname, and yes are all waiting on specs. Release 02.1 needs tee and Release 02.2 needs head, both still pending their specs.\n\nLooking at the consistency errors, there are three orphaned PRDs—prd019-seq, prd020-true, and prd021-false—that exist without matching use cases, even though the true command code is already implemented. Given the constraint that I can only propose one task, I need to figure out what's actually the earliest incomplete work. Releases 00.0, 01.0, and 01.1 all have their code in place, so they're effectively complete. I should focus on what's blocking the next release.\n\nRelease 02.0 has true in progress under issue #204, but the remaining commands—false, yes, basename, and dirname—are still pending. Since true is already being worked on, the logical next task would be implementing cmd/false, which has the orphaned prd021-false PRD available to reference.\n\nThe implementation would follow the same pattern as cmd/true but exit with status 1 instead of 0, and I'd need to include prd021-false in the required_reading to ensure the task is self-contained for the stitch agent. I need to check whether the stitch agent will have access to prd021-false through the required_reading field, since I don't have its actual contents in the project context. Looking at cmd/true as a reference, the pattern is straightforward—cmd/false would follow the same structure but return exit status 1 instead of 0. I should propose implementing cmd/false as the single task, making sure to include prd021-false in the required reading so the agent can access the full specification. about what requirements I specify.\n\nLooking at GNU false behavior:\n- Exits with status 1 unconditionally\n- Ignores all arguments except --help and --version as first argument\n- --help prints usage and exits 0\n- --version prints version and exits 0\n\nThis mirrors cmd/true exactly but with exit code 1 instead of 0.\n\nGiven the pattern from cmd/true/main.go, cmd/false would:\n1. Exit 1 by default (R1)\n2. Handle --help (first arg only) to print usage and exit 0 (R2)\n3. Handle --version (first arg only) to print version and exit 0 (R2)\n4. Silently ignore all other arguments (R3)\n5. Handle SIGPIPE (R5)\n\nFor the task description, I need to reference the PRD and provide enough context.\n\nNow I'm deciding whether to propose the implementation task first or tests—implementation comes before tests per the ordering rules. I don't see a test file for cmd/true in the source code, so tests haven't been written for that either. I'll create a single implementation task titled \"Implement cmd/false (prd021-false R1-R5)\" where the stitch agent reads the PRD and cmd/true/main.go as a reference, then creates cmd/false/main.go with exit code 1 instead of 0. Since the constraint requires splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks, I need to check whether a test file should be created as part of this task or handled separately. I'm settling on four requirements that align with the GNU false behavior: unconditional exit status 1, handling --help and --version only as the first argument, silently ignoring all other arguments, and installing a SIGPIPE handler. I'll follow the same pattern as cmd/true with parseArgs, use the existing SIGPIPE handler from pkg/sys, and leverage bufio.Writer for output. The implementation should compile cleanly and fit within the line budget at around 77 lines, with acceptance criteria covering compilation, binary creation, and the correct exit behaviors.\n\nNow I'm verifying that cmd/false is the right next step since cmd/true is already in progress, and I'm confirming I should propose only the implementation file without tests since the constraint requires splitting them into separate tasks. The task description references the correct PRD requirement IDs following the prd021-false pattern. I'm seeing that all the 02.0 use cases are pending spec and haven't started yet, but there are orphaned PRDs like prd021-false that exist outside the roadmap structure. Since cmd/true already has implementation code, I should reference that existing pattern so the stitch agent understands the codebase structure when working on the next commands.\n\nFor cmd/false, it's nearly identical to true—just swapping the exit code from 0 to 1, changing the help text to indicate failure, and updating the program name throughout. The implementation would be roughly the same length, just with those key substitutions.\n\nI should also check if there's a PRD file for false that the stitch agent references, since the consistency errors mention an orphaned prd021-false. I'll add that to the required reading along with the true implementation as a reference.\n\nNow I'm laying out the full task structure with the file to create, the specific requirements for the false utility including exit codes, help/version handling, and argument behavior, plus the validation rules that need to be met. Now I'm laying out the acceptance criteria to verify the implementation works correctly—the binary needs to compile, exit with code 1 by default, handle --help and --version flags properly with exit code 0, and reject any other arguments with exit code 1. I'm also catching myself on the constraint about not inventing design decisions beyond what the PRD specifies, so I should stick strictly to what's documented. I'm satisfied with how this is structured—the requirements stay within the limit and everything aligns well.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":87531,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":87531},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ba5c6151-9645-4b31-bc38-e5cde42fcdda","uuid":"78c0ee3b-02e5-400a-9262-cf13e92bbe61"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HpygezVMsz5PJV68oqHwau","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development that derives formal specs from C source code of classic Unix utilities, generates Go implementations from those specs using an AI agent, and verifies parity via differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) with shared libraries (`pkg/sys/` for syscalls, `pkg/format/` for output formatting, `pkg/testutils/` for differential testing) and per-utility packages under `cmd/`.\n\n**Current state**: Source code exists for `pkg/testutils`, `pkg/sys`, `pkg/format`, `cmd/cat`, `cmd/wc`, `cmd/sponge`, `cmd/du`, `cmd/true`, and `cmd/version`. All releases 00.0 through 01.3 are effectively complete (code present in source). Issue #204 (cmd/true) is in-progress. Release 02.0 is the current focus.\n\n**Current release**: Release 02.0 (trivial utilities: true, false, yes, basename, dirname). `cmd/true` exists and is in-progress via issue #204. The next use case is `rel02.0-uc002-false`. An orphaned PRD `prd021-false` exists for this utility.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nRelease 02.0 is the earliest incomplete release. Within it, `cmd/true` is already implemented (issue #204 in-progress). The next canonical item is `cmd/false` (rel02.0-uc002-false), which has an existing PRD (prd021-false). `cmd/false` is trivially similar to `cmd/true` — same structure, exit code 1 instead of 0. No dependencies on unbuilt code; it uses only `pkg/sys` which already exists.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement cmd/false (prd021-false R1-R5)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd021-false.yaml (PRD for false utility)\n      - cmd/true/main.go (reference implementation pattern for trivial exit-code utilities)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (cmd/ component structure, design decisions DD6 and DD7)\n      - pkg/sys/signal.go (InstallSIGPIPEHandler used by all cmd/ utilities)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/false/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"false utility -- exits with status 1, handles --help and --version\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Exit with status 1 unconditionally when no recognized option is the first argument (prd021-false R1). All arguments are silently ignored except --help and --version as the first argument.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Handle --help as the first argument: print usage message to stdout describing the utility and exit 0. Handle --version as the first argument: print version string to stdout and exit 0. On stdout write failure, print diagnostic to stderr and exit 1 (prd021-false R2).\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Silently ignore all arguments that are not --help or --version as the first argument, including short flags (-x), long flags (--unknown), and positional arguments (prd021-false R3).\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Install SIGPIPE handler via pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at program start for clean pipeline termination (prd021-false R5).\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow the exact structure of cmd/true/main.go. cmd/false is structurally identical to cmd/true except: progName is 'false', the default exit code is 1, and the help text says 'failure' instead of 'success'. Read cmd/true/main.go and replicate its pattern.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use bufio.NewWriter(os.Stdout) for --help and --version output, with Flush() error checking that prints a diagnostic to stderr and exits 1 on write failure, matching the cmd/true pattern.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Declare a package-level version variable with default 'dev', settable via -ldflags '-X main.version=<tag>', matching the cmd/true and cmd/version pattern.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/false/main.go compiles without errors: 'go build ./cmd/false/' succeeds with no warnings.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Binary exits 1 when invoked with no arguments.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Binary exits 0 and prints usage to stdout when invoked with --help as the first argument.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"Binary exits 0 and prints version to stdout when invoked with --version as the first argument.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"Binary exits 1 when invoked with any other arguments (e.g., -x, --unknown, foo, --help preceded by another arg).\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":87531,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":87531},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ba5c6151-9645-4b31-bc38-e5cde42fcdda","uuid":"5867d7c3-6e35-4ff4-aea1-29844967a58a"}
